From: Stained Glass OS contributors
Subject: wintrust, crypt32: PowerShell script signatures

A signed PowerShell script (.ps1, .psm1, .psd1) carries its PKCS #7
signature in "# SIG # Begin signature block" comment lines at its end.
Wine had no subject interface package for it: WinVerifyTrust answered
TRUST_E_SUBJECT_FORM_UNKNOWN, so Get-AuthenticodeSignature said
UnknownError for every signed script, the AllSigned execution policy could
not be used, and Install-Module refused every signed module from the
PowerShell Gallery ("the authenticode signature ... is not valid").

- crypt32: CryptSIPRetrieveSubjectGuid gives those extensions the
  PowerShell SIP's subject GUID {603BCC1F-4B59-4E08-B724-D2C6297EF351} (the
  one such signatures name in their SpcSipInfo).
- wintrust (psscript.c): the signed message is the block's base64; the
  digest is over the script as UTF-16LE text -- a byte-order mark the file
  has counting as its first character -- up to the line break before the
  block (established against signed modules from the PowerShell Gallery).
  CryptSIPGetSignedDataMsg and CryptSIPVerifyIndirectData serve the SIP,
  softpub checks the digest (and that the signature names this SIP), and
  the SIP is registered with the others.

Gate: test/pssig-gate.sh (scripts signed with a throwaway root by
test/pssig-make.py: valid, with a byte-order mark, tampered, unsigned).
